---
/*
  THE SITE AS A DECK OF PAGES YOU CAN SWIPE THROUGH.

  Chris: "could we make these swipeable like pages on an app? Or no? And on
  desktop, we'd have to find some kind of equivalent workaround."

  WHAT THIS IS NOT

  Not a carousel. Holding all five pages in one document is the obvious way
  to get a real swipe and it is the wrong one here: it collapses five URLs
  into one, takes four pages out of the index, and makes every visitor
  download the 82-tile Portfolio grid to read the About page. For a site
  whose job is presenting to agencies, losing the URLs is a bigger loss than
  the gesture is a gain.

  So a swipe is just a second way to trigger a link that already exists. The
  URL changes, back and forward work, each page stays its own document.

  WHY NO CLIENT-SIDE ROUTER

  Astro's ClientRouter would give a better feel - same-document swaps, no
  load at all - and it was rejected on blast radius. It turns every link on
  the site into a client-side swap, which means every script has to survive
  being re-run or re-initialised per navigation: Isotope's masonry and its
  patched column placement, the constellation, the tile sharpening, the
  autoplay observers, InkMode's source swapping. That is the machinery most
  recently worked on and the most expensive to break subtly.

  Native cross-document view transitions need none of it. `@view-transition`
  is a CSS opt-in; the BROWSER animates between two ordinary document loads.
  Every script keeps running exactly when it does today, because each page is
  still a full load. If the feel turns out not to be good enough, the router
  is still available as a second step - but it should be a decision taken
  after feeling this, not before.

  HONEST LIMITS

  - The slide needs `@view-transition` (Chrome 126+, Safari 18.2+). Older
    browsers get an ordinary page change: the swipe still navigates, it just
    does not animate. Nothing is broken, it is only less pretty.
  - The adjacent pages' HTML is prefetched, which is cheap. Their IMAGES are
    not, so a swipe is fast rather than instant. Prerendering would make it
    instant and would cost a full load of both neighbours - on a phone, that
    is the Portfolio grid downloaded to read About. Not worth it unasked.

  PREVIEW ONLY, like the constellation and the collage: a Sanity publish
  deploys main to production, so an experiment must not reach the live site
  by a route nobody chose.
*/
export interface Props {
  /** The deck, in order. Internal paths only. */
  pages: {href: string; label: string}[];
  /** The path of the page this is rendering on. */
  here: string;
}

const {pages, here} = Astro.props;

/*
  Trailing slashes differ between the built file and the served URL, so both
  are normalised before comparing. Getting this wrong silently puts every
  page at index -1, which disables the deck everywhere while looking fine.
*/
const norm = (p: string) => (p === '/' ? '/' : p.replace(/\/+$/, ''));
const at = pages.findIndex((p) => norm(p.href) === norm(here));

const prev = at > 0 ? pages[at - 1] : null;
const next = at >= 0 && at < pages.length - 1 ? pages[at + 1] : null;
const live = at >= 0 && (prev || next);
---

{live && (
  <>
    {/*
      HTML only, deliberately. See the note above on why the images are left
      to stream rather than prerendered.
    */}
    {prev && <link rel="prefetch" href={prev.href} />}
    {next && <link rel="prefetch" href={next.href} />}

    {/*
      The desktop answer, and it is not a gesture.

      A trackpad two-finger swipe is detectable and feels native on a Mac,
      but it is invisible to anyone on a mouse and it fights the same
      sideways-scrolling media rows the touch gesture has to avoid. So
      desktop gets something honest instead: a labelled edge affordance and
      the arrow keys.

      Naming the destination is the point. It makes the order of the deck
      visible, which is what lets the phone gesture feel learned rather than
      stumbled into - and on its own it is just decent navigation.
    */}
    <nav class="deck" aria-label="Page sequence">
      {prev && (
        <a class="deck__edge deck__edge--prev" href={prev.href} data-deck-prev rel="prev">
          <span class="deck__arrow" aria-hidden="true">&#8592;</span>
          <span class="deck__label">{prev.label}</span>
        </a>
      )}
      {next && (
        <a class="deck__edge deck__edge--next" href={next.href} data-deck-next rel="next">
          <span class="deck__label">{next.label}</span>
          <span class="deck__arrow" aria-hidden="true">&#8594;</span>
        </a>
      )}
    </nav>

    <script is:inline define:vars={{prevHref: prev?.href ?? null, nextHref: next?.href ?? null}}>
      (() => {
        /*
          Direction is remembered across the navigation.

          A cross-document transition means the incoming page is a different
          document and cannot know which way the visitor went. sessionStorage
          survives the hop; `pagereveal` on the new page reads it, tags the
          transition, and clears it so a later reload or a nav click does not
          inherit a stale direction.
        */
        const KEY = 'deck-dir';
        const go = (href, dir) => {
          if (!href) return;
          try {
            sessionStorage.setItem(KEY, dir);
          } catch {}
          location.assign(href);
        };

        /* Tag the OUTGOING side, for browsers that run it. */
        addEventListener('pageswap', (e) => {
          if (!e.viewTransition) return;
          let dir = null;
          try {
            dir = sessionStorage.getItem(KEY);
          } catch {}
          if (dir) e.viewTransition.types.add(`deck-${dir}`);
        });

        /* Tag the INCOMING side, which is the one that actually animates. */
        addEventListener('pagereveal', (e) => {
          if (!e.viewTransition) return;
          let dir = null;
          try {
            dir = sessionStorage.getItem(KEY);
            sessionStorage.removeItem(KEY);
          } catch {}
          if (dir) e.viewTransition.types.add(`deck-${dir}`);
        });

        /*
          WHEN A SWIPE IS NOT OURS.

          Three conflicts, all real, and the rules are deliberately
          conservative - a page that changes when you did not mean it to is
          far worse than a gesture that sometimes needs a second go.
        */

        /*
          1. The sideways media rows. A flick inside one scrolls the row.

          Any swipe STARTING inside a horizontal scroller is ignored
          outright, rather than ignored only while the row has somewhere left
          to go. Flicking to the end of a row and tipping over into a page
          change on the next flick would be maddening, and "it depends how
          far you already scrolled" is not a rule anyone can learn.
        */
        const inSideScroller = (node) => {
          let el = node instanceof Element ? node : null;
          while (el && el !== document.body) {
            const ox = getComputedStyle(el).overflowX;
            if ((ox === 'auto' || ox === 'scroll') && el.scrollWidth > el.clientWidth + 8) return true;
            el = el.parentElement;
          }
          return false;
        };

        /*
          2. Anything already holding the gesture, or mid-interaction.

          An expanded portfolio tile is a visitor looking at one picture; the
          open mobile menu is a visitor choosing a page by hand. Swiping the
          whole site out from under either is an interruption.
        */
        const busy = () =>
          !!document.querySelector('.pf-item.is-expanded') ||
          !!document.querySelector('.site-nav__links.is-open') ||
          !!document.querySelector('[aria-expanded="true"].site-nav__toggle');

        /* 3. iOS Safari claims horizontal swipes near the screen edges for
           its own back and forward, so the gesture lives in the middle. */
        const EDGE_GUARD = 32;
        const AXIS_RATIO = 1.8;

        /*
          THE PAGE FOLLOWS YOUR THUMB, AND THAT IS BOTH FIXES AT ONCE.

          Chris had two complaints about the first version and they have one
          answer. "It's too easy across any page to have a left or a right
          swipe just immediately change the page... I'm okay introducing one
          stop of friction so that we don't make a mistake swiping." And:
          "there's no animation currently, whereas I think this might be the
          one time where an animation would be quite useful to feel like
          you've shifted left or right between the pages."

          A distance threshold would have answered the first and done
          nothing for the second - a bigger invisible number is still
          invisible, and you would still not know you were swiping until the
          page had already gone. Dragging the page with the finger answers
          both: you SEE it move, which is the shift he wants to feel, and
          you can change your mind, which is the friction. Past the commit
          point it goes; short of it, it springs back. Nothing is a mistake
          you cannot take back mid-gesture.

          It also makes the animation independent of view transitions, which
          is why there was none: @view-transition needs Safari 18.2+ and the
          `:active-view-transition-type()` selector is newer still, so on
          Chris's phone the slide was simply never running. The drag is
          transforms and runs everywhere. The view transition, where it
          exists, now only has to cover the swap at the end.

          COMMIT is deliberately a share of the screen rather than a fixed
          pixel count, so the gesture means the same thing on a phone and on
          a tablet.
        */
        const COMMIT = 0.3;
        const RESIST = 0.45;

        /*
          <main>, not <body>.

          Transforming the body would make it a containing block for every
          position:fixed descendant, which on this site means the nav header
          and the deck's own edge affordances would start sliding with the
          content and detach from the viewport.

          Moving only <main> is both safer and better: the chrome stays
          nailed down and the content slides underneath it, which is how an
          app pager actually behaves.
        */
        const skin = document.querySelector('main');
        if (!skin) return;

        let sx = 0;
        let sy = 0;
        let axis = null;
        let armed = false;
        let dragging = false;

        /*
          A page pulled sideways is wider than the viewport, and without
          this the browser offers a horizontal scrollbar for the empty space
          it was dragged into. Clipped only while a drag is live, so nothing
          about the page's normal overflow behaviour changes.
        */
        const clip = (on) => {
          document.documentElement.style.overflowX = on ? 'clip' : '';
        };

        const paint = (dx) => {
          clip(true);
          skin.style.transition = 'none';
          skin.style.transform = `translate3d(${dx}px,0,0)`;
        };

        const release = () => {
          dragging = false;
          skin.style.transition = 'transform 260ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)';
          skin.style.transform = '';
          setTimeout(() => {
            skin.style.transition = '';
            clip(false);
          }, 300);
        };

        addEventListener(
          'touchstart',
          (e) => {
            armed = false;
            axis = null;
            dragging = false;
            if (e.touches.length !== 1) return;
            const t = e.touches[0];
            if (t.clientX < EDGE_GUARD || t.clientX > innerWidth - EDGE_GUARD) return;
            if (busy() || inSideScroller(e.target)) return;
            sx = t.clientX;
            sy = t.clientY;
            armed = true;
          },
          {passive: true},
        );

        addEventListener(
          'touchmove',
          (e) => {
            if (!armed || e.touches.length !== 1) return;
            const dx = e.touches[0].clientX - sx;
            const dy = e.touches[0].clientY - sy;

            /*
              The axis is locked once, on the first movement big enough to
              have a direction, and never revisited. Deciding it per-frame
              lets a slow diagonal drag flip between scrolling and
              navigating, which feels like the page is arguing.
            */
            if (!axis) {
              if (Math.abs(dx) < 12 && Math.abs(dy) < 12) return;
              axis = Math.abs(dx) > Math.abs(dy) * AXIS_RATIO ? 'x' : 'y';
              if (axis !== 'x') return;
            }
            if (axis !== 'x') return;

            /*
              Dragging toward a page that is not there still moves, at less
              than half rate. That rubber-band is the standard way of saying
              "this is the end" - going rigid reads as a broken gesture,
              and Chris asked for exactly this feeling on the media rows.
            */
            const wanted = dx < 0 ? nextHref : prevHref;
            dragging = true;
            paint(wanted ? dx : dx * RESIST);
          },
          {passive: true},
        );

        addEventListener(
          'touchend',
          (e) => {
            if (!armed || axis !== 'x') {
              if (dragging) release();
              return;
            }
            armed = false;
            const dx = e.changedTouches[0].clientX - sx;
            const far = Math.abs(dx) >= innerWidth * COMMIT;
            // Swiping left pulls the next page in from the right, which is
            // the direction every app-like pager agrees on.
            const wanted = dx < 0 ? nextHref : prevHref;

            if (far && wanted) {
              /*
                Carry the page the rest of the way out before the
                navigation, so the gesture finishes rather than jumping. The
                document load starts immediately underneath it - this is a
                paint, not a wait.
              */
              skin.style.transition = 'transform 180ms ease-out';
              skin.style.transform = `translate3d(${dx < 0 ? -innerWidth : innerWidth}px,0,0)`;
              go(wanted, dx < 0 ? 'forward' : 'back');
              return;
            }
            release();
          },
          {passive: true},
        );

        /* A cancelled touch - a call, a notification - must not leave the
           page parked off to one side. */
        addEventListener(
          'touchcancel',
          () => {
            armed = false;
            axis = null;
            if (dragging) release();
          },
          {passive: true},
        );

        /*
          The keyboard half of the desktop answer.

          Guarded against firing while someone is typing, and against
          hijacking a browser shortcut. An arrow key inside a form field, or
          with a modifier held, belongs to the field or the browser.
        */
        addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
          if (e.key !== 'ArrowLeft' && e.key !== 'ArrowRight') return;
          if (e.metaKey || e.ctrlKey || e.altKey || e.shiftKey) return;
          const el = document.activeElement;
          const tag = el?.tagName;
          if (tag === 'INPUT' || tag === 'TEXTAREA' || tag === 'SELECT' || tag === 'IFRAME') return;
          if (el instanceof HTMLElement && el.isContentEditable) return;
          if (busy()) return;
          if (e.key === 'ArrowRight') go(nextHref, 'forward');
          else go(prevHref, 'back');
        });
      })();
    </script>

    <style is:global>
      /*
        The browser animates between two real document loads. Scoped to this
        component's own style block rather than global.css so the whole
        mechanism only exists where the deck renders - which is preview.
      */
      @view-transition {
        navigation: auto;
      }

      /*
        Reduced motion gets the navigation with no slide. The default
        cross-fade the browser would otherwise apply is still motion, so the
        animations are only defined inside the no-preference query.
      */
      @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
        html:active-view-transition-type(deck-forward)::view-transition-old(root) {
          animation: deck-out-left 300ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1) both;
        }
        html:active-view-transition-type(deck-forward)::view-transition-new(root) {
          animation: deck-in-right 300ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1) both;
        }
        html:active-view-transition-type(deck-back)::view-transition-old(root) {
          animation: deck-out-right 300ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1) both;
        }
        html:active-view-transition-type(deck-back)::view-transition-new(root) {
          animation: deck-in-left 300ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1) both;
        }
      }

      @keyframes deck-out-left {
        to {
          transform: translateX(-28%);
          opacity: 0;
        }
      }
      @keyframes deck-in-right {
        from {
          transform: translateX(100%);
        }
      }
      @keyframes deck-out-right {
        to {
          transform: translateX(28%);
          opacity: 0;
        }
      }
      @keyframes deck-in-left {
        from {
          transform: translateX(-100%);
        }
      }

      /*
        THE EDGE AFFORDANCE.

        Fixed, vertically centred, and quiet until hovered. It carries the
        destination's name because an unlabelled arrow asks the visitor to
        guess what order the site is in.

        Hidden on a phone: there the gesture is the affordance, and two
        floating chips over a masonry grid is clutter on the screen with the
        least room for it.
      */
      .deck {
        display: none;
      }

      @media (min-width: 900px) {
        .deck {
          display: block;
        }

        .deck__edge {
          position: fixed;
          top: 50%;
          transform: translateY(-50%);
          z-index: 40;
          display: flex;
          align-items: center;
          gap: 0.5rem;
          padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
          background: var(--color-cream, #f6f1e7);
          border: 1px solid var(--color-navy, #1b2a4a);
          color: var(--color-navy, #1b2a4a);
          text-decoration: none;
          font-size: 0.8125rem;
          letter-spacing: 0.04em;
          text-transform: uppercase;
          opacity: 0.35;
          transition: opacity 180ms ease, transform 180ms ease;
        }

        .deck__edge--prev {
          left: 0;
          border-left: 0;
        }
        .deck__edge--next {
          right: 0;
          border-right: 0;
        }

        .deck__edge:hover,
        .deck__edge:focus-visible {
          opacity: 1;
        }

        /*
          The label is the part that costs room, so at rest only the arrow
          shows and the name arrives on hover. Collapsed with a max-width
          rather than display:none so it stays in the accessibility tree and
          a screen reader still reads "About" rather than an arrow glyph.
        */
        .deck__label {
          max-width: 0;
          overflow: hidden;
          white-space: nowrap;
          transition: max-width 220ms ease;
        }

        .deck__edge:hover .deck__label,
        .deck__edge:focus-visible .deck__label {
          max-width: 12rem;
        }

        @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
          .deck__edge,
          .deck__label {
            transition: none;
          }
        }
      }
    </style>
  </>
)}
